<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(Creativity, Copyright, and Chaos)/E(Creativity, Copyright, and Chaos)/Pg 12 0 R/K[0]/P 74 0 R>>
endobj

163 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/E(The ability for AI to generate unlimited videos, images, and text of any kind in an instant has flooded the internet. Social media feeds are increasingly becoming littered with AI content, books on Amazon contain AI, and an increasing amount of online sellers have been found using AI in merchandise. It begs the question: With unlimited content and increasing indistinguishable-from-reality AI content, what happens next?)/Pg 12 0 R/K[2]/P 74 0 R>>
endobj

164 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/E(Niche content could serve users more than current content. Picture this: shows targeted for your needs regarding plot and specified niche content. Sound sci-fi? Well, something is already in the works. Showrunner.xyz allows users to create shows with a few prompts. Dubbed the "Netflix of AI," this service makes content creation interactive and personal. )/Pg 12 0 R/K[3]/P 74 0 R>>
endobj

165 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/E(While not perfect, it provides a glimpse into what advancements could be made in this area. Content oversaturation could lead to the slow replacement of original works, although some experts believe AI could be used more as a tool than a replacement. )/Pg 12 0 R/K[4]/P 74 0 R>>
endobj

166 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/E(AI video has expanded massively, with new video models such as SORA and VEO 2 obliterating previous attempts. AI-polished content could create some confusing questions. Could AI-generated content be worthy of copyright? How do we distinguish what is eligible for copyright in a world where AI can generate similar content? If AI content were copyrighted, who would own AI-generated content? The user, the tool's creator, or no one?)/Pg 12 0 R/K[5]/P 74 0 R>>
endobj

