190 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/E(AI Agents vs. Artificial General Intelligence)/Pg 14 0 R/K[0]/P 76 0 R>>
endobj

191 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/E(Artificial General Intelligence \(AGI\) is a widely discussed area in AI. It refers to the goal of AI being capable of adapting to new situations regardless of prior programming for the specific task, adapting to situations like a human can. )/Pg 14 0 R/K[1]/P 76 0 R>>
endobj

192 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/E(AI agents, on the other hand, perform particular tasks by following the rules they are programmed with but cannot think for themselves, operating solely within the instructions they have been given.)/Pg 14 0 R/K[2]/P 76 0 R>>
endobj

193 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/E(AI agents are more sophisticated than regular AI chatbots because they can understand and solve complex problems. For example, an AI agent can determine users' needs and take actionable steps to help them achieve their goals without constant user authorisation.)/Pg 14 0 R/K[3]/P 76 0 R>>
endobj

194 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/E(OpenAI plans to release its first AI operator this month as a research preview. This will require it to take action independently, raising questions about whether this could be the first step toward AGI.)/Pg 14 0 R/K[4]/P 76 0 R>>
endobj

195 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/E(For more on the challenges of AGI development, see our discussion on the limits of AI learning in The Mind of Machines \(pg. X\).)/Pg 14 0 R/K[5]/P 76 0 R>>
endobj

